Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:06.580
How do you define AI?

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:15.260
Oh, that's a controversial
question, I feel like.

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:16.640
This is a pretty hard question.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:21.620
I think AI is definitely
an overloaded term,

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:25.760
even amongst researchers
who supposedly research AI.

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:28.910
AI-- artificial intelligence--

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:32.810
is the idea that we
can teach a computer

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:36.980
to replicate some things,
or eventually all things

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:39.180
that humans consider
intelligence.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:41.510
I think the term AI gets
a little bit overloaded,

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:43.670
especially in the news.

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:45.380
So for me, when
most people say AI,

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:47.150
they actually mean
machine learning,

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:49.910
which is just a pattern
recognition from seeing

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:51.500
a whole bunch of previous data.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:56.000
I think AI is the culmination
of any sorts of techniques that

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:02.420
allow computers to
solve complicated tasks

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:06.200
without humans having to
manually specify solutions

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:08.150
or really precise algorithms.

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:13.040
AI is, essentially, I like
to think of intelligence

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:15.770
as the ability to
process information

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:18.560
to inform future
decisions, and AI

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:21.470
is the ability to build
artificial algorithms

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:22.580
to do exactly that.

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:25.540
Process information to
inform future decisions.

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:29.280
I'm still trying to
figure that one out.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:29.780
Yep.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:32.830
[MUSIC PLAYING]

